 { In the last section we covered a lot of examples involving the king. This is inevitable no matter what the focus of any course is. After all, if the game reaches its later stages, the king usually gets involved. In this section we'll go over the most important positional ideas and patterns to keep in mind whenever it's time for the king to emerge from his slumber! Our first position shows an equal endgame, and we'll take a look at how White went wrong. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $6 { Violating the principle we spoke about in the previous section. White has all their central pawns on dark squares, so it was important to think about shifting them to light squares rather than placing another pawn on a dark square. } ( 1. c4 $1 { was the most positional approach. After } b5 2. b3 $1 bxc4 3. bxc4 { The position is completely equal, as the White pawns do a great job of holding back the knight. Things should simplify soon, for example: } fxg4 4. Kxg4 f6 5. Bf4 Na8 $1 6. exf6 gxf6 7. Bb8 a6 8. Ba7 $1 { The bishop cooperates with the pawns to restrict the dangerous Black knight. } Nc7 9. Kf4 $11 { Since White can hold the knight at bay and Black's structure is inaccessible, the position is drawn. } ) 
b5 $6 { Black does not punish the mistake. } ( 1... fxg4 $1 { was best, with the point being that after } 2. Kxg4 Kd5 3. Kf3 b5 $17 { we can see how Black is arranging their army on light squares, to try to exploit the opponent's weakness along that color complex. A very challenging practical defense lies ahead for White. } ) 
2. h5 $4 { The losing move. } ( { Here White could have ensured a draw with } 2. gxf5 $1 Kxf5 3. Ke3 $1 Kg4 { The h-pawn is sacrificed, in order to mobilise the central pawn majority. } 4. b3 Kxh4 5. c4 bxc4 6. bxc4 Kg4 7. Ba5 { Targeting the knight, so that d5 can be played next. } Ne6 8. d5 cxd5 9. cxd5 { The pawns are too strong for Black to think about pressing for a win. However, at the same time they do not have any problems and can even immediately force a draw with } Kf5 $1 { with the point being that after } 10. dxe6 Kxe6 11. Ke4 f6 $11 { The endgame is drawn, because of the rule that states that bishop of the opposite color of the queening square of flank (a- or h-) pawn is a draw. } ) 
fxg4+ $1 3. Kxg4 gxh5+ $1 { The sequence of pawn exchanges drags the king to the flank. } 4. Kxh5 Kf5 $1 { Now the Black king can easily slice through the undefended light squares. } 5. Bg5 Ne6 6. Be7 Ke4 7. Kg4 Kd3 8. Kf3 Kc2 9. Ba3 Kb1 10. Ke4 Kxa2 $19 { Black has won a pawn already, with the light square weaknesses remaining a fatal problem for White. We can see that the problem that White ran into here was to not take into account their structural weakness on the light squares, leading to misplaying the bishop vs knight endgame and allowing Black's king to cross into enemy territory. } 
