 { The question of which king can be first to activate itself and enter into the enemy position is one that occurs frequently. In this online game, Magnus Carlsen had the White pieces against GM Darius Swiercz and won effortlessly after a serise of exchanges. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxe7 $1 Rxe7 2. Rd1 { Grabbing the open file. Note how most of White's pawns are arranged on the right color while Black's pawns sit on light squares, a problem when you have the light-squared bishop. } Rd7 ( { Sitting and waiting with } 2... Re8 { would not avoid defeat. For example: } 3. Rd6 Re7 4. Kd4 Re8 5. Bg2 Re7 6. Bf1 $18 { The pawn on c4 will be captured next. } ) 
3. Rxd7 $1 Bxd7 4. Kd4 Bb5 5. Kc5 { The Black king cannot cover the d6 square due to the need to keep the b7 pawn defended. } h6 6. Kd6 $18 { The f7 pawn will fall soon, with an easily winning endgame. } 
