 { So far we have only seen the king's involvement with some minor pieces remaining. The activity of the king is just as important in cases when there are no pieces remaining. In this position, can you find the only move that avoids defeat for Black? } 1... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Correct! Black needs to break down the structure in order to avoid defeat in this complex pawn ending. } ( { In the game Black immediately went wrong with } 1... Ke6 $2 { when after } 2. a5 $18 { the problem that Black faced was that now White's queenside pawn structure could no longer be undermined. After } Kf5 3. Kf3 g5 4. fxg5 hxg5 5. h3 $1 { Robbing Black of ...g4+ possibilities. } Ke5 { Black has to keep guarding the e4 square to stop 6.Ke4. } 6. Kg4 Kf6 7. Kh5 $1 { The king continues to invade, again on the weak light squares. } Kf5 8. g3 Kf6 9. g4 g6 10. Kh6 $18 { Finally Black runs out of moves. Next, White will capture on g5. } ) 
2. bxa5 Ke6 { Now the King will march towards the c5 pawn. } 3. Kf3 Kd5 4. g3 Kxc5 { Black wins the pawn, but White has enough king activity of his own to hold the balance. } 5. Ke4 $1 Kb4 6. Kxd4 Kxa5 { Black has nothing better than to enter a pawn race. This is the domain of calculation and not positional play, as both players battle for which player has time on their side. In this case, each king is equally fast. Let's show how play would unfold: } 7. Kc5 Kxa4 8. Kb6 Kb4 9. Kxb7 Kc5 10. Kc7 Kd5 11. Kd7 c5 12. Ke7 Kd4 13. Kd6 $1 { White would lose the race against the c-pawn, but now it will be exchanged off. } Kxd3 14. Kxc5 $11 { The endgame is drawn. A quick rule of thumb to check this is by counting the total number of squares left for each king to grab the pawns. In Black's case it's Ke4-f3-g2-h2-g3-f4 for a total of six moves. In White's case it's d5-e6-f7-g7-g6-h6, for the same total of six moves. King and pawn endgames are complex, with their own set of very clearly defined rules. The topic is outside the scope of this course, but for those of you who are seeking to take your game to the next level I would strongly recommend to do a deep dive on this topic until you are confident that you have mastered the fundamentals. Guessing your way through pawn endgames is a road to disaster! } 
