 { Another positional idea relating to king activity is a defensive one. Here we have a game where Magnus Carlsen is quick to use his king to defend against Black's main positional threat. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf2 $1 f5 2. Ke3 $1 { In the starting position White spotted that Black's powerful idea involved the pawn break on f4. To identify the threat, White recognised two things: the opponent's 4 vs 3 pawn majority on the kingside; and the fact that Black has the light-squared bishop (meaning that they will generally want to place their pawns on dark squares). } Kf6 3. Nd5+ { Transitioning to a drawn rook and pawn endgame. } Bxd5 4. cxd5 Ke7 5. h5 $1 { Breaking up the dangerous pawn majority. } gxh5 6. exf5 Kf6 7. d6 Kxf5 8. d7 Ra8 { White is down a pawn for now, but his piece activity means he is not in any trouble. } 9. Rh1 Kg6 10. Ra1 $1 { Exploiting the fact that Black must guard against the passed pawn. } Rd8 11. Ra6 Rxd7 12. Rxb6+ Kg5 13. Re6 Kf5 14. Rc6 $11 { With a drawn rook endgame, though if either side must be careful it is Black as the b-pawn is the most advanced. In this game we saw how Magnus spotted that his king was needed in order to stop the opponent's plan, centered around the pawn break on f4. } 
