 { In the last example, we saw the king moving towards the centre of the board in order to stop the positional threat. This is frequently necessary. In this position, White recognises that their king is misplaced on g2, and so embarks on a journey to a better square! } 1. { -KEY- } Kf1 $1 ( 1. b4 $6 { If White had rushed without their king, they would lose all of their advantage. For example: } Kd6 $1 { Black centralises their own king. } 2. Rac1 h4 3. c5 bxc5 4. bxc5 Ke5 $1 5. c6 Bc8 6. Rhd1 hxg3 7. fxg3 Rh4 { Threatening ...Rxf4. } 8. Nh3 Rxh3 { There are a few ways to hold the balance for Black, but this move is perhaps the simplest. } 9. Kxh3 f4 10. Kh4 Rxg3 11. d6 Rh3 12. Kg5 Rg3 13. Kh5 Rh3 14. Kg6 Rg3 { with a draw, since the move } 15. Kf7 $4 { would lose on the spot to } Be6 $1 16. Ke8 Rg8 17. Ke7 Rg7 18. Kf8 Kf6 19. Rg1 Rxg1 20. Rxg1 bxc6 $19 { Black's connected pawns are too strong. } ) 
Kd6 2. Ke2 $1 Ke5 3. Ke3 $16 { White has improved their king as much as possible, and is also stopping further activation of the Black king. They stand much better here since the h5 pawn is weak with Black's pawns arranged poorly along light squares. From here White can try to play b4 and c5 to mobilise their majority. Additionally, they might play Rad1 and d6, when the threat of Rd5+ could be a serious problem for Black. } 
