 { This example again requires finding the right maneuver with the king. This example is really advanced from start to finish, but it contains a lot of beautiful ideas so I hope you enjoy working your way through it as much as I did. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf1 $1 { In the previous example, the king ran from g2 to the centre. On this occasion, the opposite approach is needed! } ( 1. Kd2 $6 { is also a logical try, and was in fact Carlsen's choice in the game. However, after } Rh3 $1 { Black is holding the king back. } 2. Rf1 Be8 $11 { Black just sits and waits, and since their pieces and pawns all are ideally placed, it is not possible for White to make any progress in spite of the extra pawn. Note how in this example, Black has pawns perfectly arranged in harmony with their light-squared bishop. } ) 
Rh1+ 2. Kg2 $1 { The White king was not in time to prevent the Black rook from reaching the back rank, but due to White's passed f-pawn it is not possible for the rook to stick around. } Rh8 { A forced retreat. } ( 2... Rc1 $2 3. f6 $18 { The pawn will cost Black their bishop after } Be8 4. f7 { . } ) 
3. Kg3 $1 Rh1 4. Bf3 { Kicking it out once again! } Rh8 { Black is forced to sit and wait. } ( 4... Rc1 $2 5. Rh2 Be8 6. Rh8 Bf7 7. Rh7 Be8 8. Rxc7 $18 { It turns out that it is White's rook that does far more damage following an invasion along the h-file! } ) 
5. Rf1 $1 ( 5. Rh2 $6 { is premature, when after } Rxh2 6. Kxh2 Kf4 $1 { Black's newfound king activity on f4 makes White's winning task much more challenging. } ) 
Rh7 6. Rh1 $1 { Now it's a different story. The rooks are exchanged without conceding the f4 square. } Rxh1 7. Bxh1 Be8 { Black sits and waits, challenging White to find a winning configuration. This is where schematic thinking comes into play. If you wish you can pause here and consider what you think White's plan should be. Remember that a schema involves two things: (1) finding the ideal piece configuration; (2) identifying the moves that will get you there. There may be more than one way to convert from here, but I will show the schema that I think makes the most sense. If you considered a different approach, it may be a cool idea to check if that was also a winning path by analysing it with the engine. Over time, such practices will help you to develop your intuition and technique. } 8. Bf3 $1 { The first step of the schema is to bring the bishop to c2. From here it will control the forward jumps of the knight and pressure the a4 pawn. } Bd7 9. Bd1 Be8 10. Bc2 Bd7 11. Nb1 $1 { The second stage of the plan. White aims the knight towards f3 to disrupt Black's blockade along the dark squares. } b5 { Schematic thinking gets more complicated when the opponent has options besides sitting around idle. This is where calculation comes into play. } ( { Black cannot afford to continue sitting back any longer, since after } 11... Be8 12. Nd2 Bd7 13. Nf3 Kh6 14. Kh4 $18 { the connected pawns will soon begin to march forward. } ) 
12. Nd2 bxc4 13. Nf3+ $1 { White accepts the loss of the c4 pawn in order to get the connected pawns rolling as soon as possible. } Kf6 14. g5+ Kf7 15. g6+ $1 { This move is a 'bad' positional move in that the pawns should usually be marched forward on the opposite color of the bishop. However, concrete calculation will always trump generalities, and here White has spotted a way to break the dark-squared blockade. } Kf6 16. Ng5 $3 { The knight is immune since ...Kxg5 is met by g7 when the pawn will promote on the next move. } Nd3 { Black targets the b2 pawn. } 17. Nh7+ Kg7 18. Kg4 { Now the king is able to march forward to the g5 square. } e4 19. Kg5 Bxf5 { White's play culminates in Black having to give up their bishop. } 20. Kxf5 e3 { White has to find one more precise sequence: } 21. Ng5 $1 e2 22. Nf3 $1 e1=Q 23. Nxe1 Nxe1 { Black has recovered their missing piece, but fortunately for White the resulting bishop vs knight endgame is winning with precise play. } 24. Bxa4 Nd3 25. Bc2 $1 { The final critical move. } ( 25. Ke4 $4 { In chess, it's often 'never too late' to blunder away the full point! } Nc5 $1 ) 
Nxb2 26. Ke4 { Now the king can safely return. } Kxg6 27. a4 $18 { The knight is stuck on b2, and the Black king is too far to catch the pawn. Black will have to give up their knight for the pawn, leaving White with a winning endgame. } 
