 { The final game in the course! Well done to everyone who worked their way through the material, I am sure it's been challenging but I really hope that overall it's been an enjoyable process and that it's helped you on your path to developing into a strong positional player. We'll close out the section with an old classic, the battle between two of the leading players of their time, Siegbert Tarrasch and Richard Teichmann. The game was played in the famous tournament of San Sebastian at the start of the 20th Century. We kick things off on move 18, with White having a better position due to his superior minor pieces and structure. } 18. { -KEY- } Kf2 $1 { With no queens or dark-squared bishops on the board, the time is right for the king to enter the game. } Rc7 19. Ke3 Re8 20. Rf2 { The bishop on g2 is biting on granite, so White lifts his rook to be able to maneuver it on to the f1-a6 diagonal. } Nb7 21. Bf1 Na5 { The knight is hoping to find an active post on c4. } 22. b3 $1 { Paralysing the knight. } h6 23. Bd3 Nc6 { Black finds nothing better than to offer a knight exchange. White is glad to accept the trade, as this will allow his king to progress further up the board. } 24. Nxc6 Bxc6 25. Kd4 $16 { The difference in the pawn structure is evident. Black lacks activity for his light-squared bishop, as well as being saddled with chronic dark square weaknesses. Already, Black is facing a near-impossible defensive task. } Bd7 26. g4 $1 { With his army well-configured, White prepares a kingside pawn break to open new lines of attack. } Bc8 27. h4 g6 $6 { Black braces himself for the pawn storm, but now the g6 pawn becomes an easy target to latch on to. } 28. Rh1 $1 Kg7 29. h5 $1 Rh8 30. Rfh2 { As covered in the chapter on open & semi-open files, we can see that White benefits from being the side that 'gets to choose' if and when to open up the h-file. } Bd7 31. g5 $6 ( 31. Rf1 $1 { Pivoting away from the h-file and looking for the break on f5 was even stronger, though a little unhuman given that White had just placed his rook on h1. } Rc6 32. hxg6 fxg6 33. Rg2 { The x-ray against the Black king will add further potency to the f5 break. } Rf8 34. f5 $1 { White crashes through violently. For example: } exf5 35. gxf5 g5 36. f6 $18 { Black is helpless against the connected passed pawns. } ) 
hxg5 32. fxg5 Rxh5 33. Rxh5 gxh5 34. Rxh5 { The result of the exchange of rooks and pawns is that White is now ready to invade along the h-file. } Kf8 ( 34... Rc8 35. Rh7 Kg8 36. g6 Be8 37. Ke3 $1 { If Black does nothing, the White king will run up to f6 to add decisive pressure against f7. } fxg6 { Black has nothing better than to take, but now the White rook's power is unleashed along the 7th rank. } 38. Re7 Bf7 { The only way to avoid the loss of the e6 pawn, but now White's king decides: } 39. Kf4 $1 Kg7 40. Kg5 $18 { Black is lost, since White's threat is to play 41.Bxg6, while the move } Kf8 { can be met by } 41. Kf6 $1 ) 
35. Rh8+ Ke7 36. g6 $1 { Opening up a final set of lines. } fxg6 37. Bxg6 b4 38. Rh7+ ( 38. Bd3 $1 { was best, with the point being that after } a5 39. Ra8 $18 { there is no way to defend the pawn. } ) 
Kd8 39. Bd3 $6 { Having played fantastically throughout the endgame, White plays an inaccuracy at the final hurdle. } Rc3 $2 { Under pressure, Black makes the final mistake. } ( 39... Rc6 $13 { Black needed to have taken the lifeline that White had given him. Here it would have been very challenging to break down his defenses, since the rook does a great job of protecting the vulnerable a6 and e6 pawns while also locking the enemy king out of play. } ) 
40. a3 $1 { A final pawn break unravels the Black position. } a5 41. Rh8+ $1 Ke7 42. Ra8 { The point! Now the a5 pawn cannot be defended, and so the Black queenside will soon collapse. In view of this, Black threw in the towel and resigned. I hope you enjoyed this game, which was chosen as the final game of the chapter, as it illustrates several of the themes that we have explored across the various sections. White applied strong schematic thinking throughout the game to find the ideal configuration to aim for at every turn. The king played a pivotal role throughout, while the problems for Black were rooted on the poor harmony between his light-squared bishop and the pawn formation. Of course, there is a lot more that could be covered when it comes to endgame strategy. Nevertheless, I hope that you have enjoyed and benefited from our coverage of some of the more important and frequently occurring positional concepts and strategies that you will encounter at the chessboard. And... That's it! From here there remains only the chapter summary, and a final mixed puzzle set. I hope you've enjoyed this course, and best of luck with your chess journey moving forward. Lastly, if you would like to share your experiences with the course with me, I would love to hear it! You can reach out to me at astanehchess@gmail.com or leave a review of the course directly on the Chessable platform. Until the next time! } 
 1-0
